.TH STYXLOG 1
.SH NAME
styxlog \- what a recorded session did
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B styxlog
[
.B -d
]
[
.B -b
.I nbytes
]
[
.B -f
.I substring
]
.I tracefile
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Styxlog
reads a trace written by
.B iostats -t
(see
.IR iostats (4))
and prints what the session did, as opens, reads and writes against paths,
with the time each happened.
.PP
A raw trace is a sequence of messages about numbered fids, which is the
wrong vocabulary for reading: nothing in it says that the program opened
.BR /dis/wm/toolbar.dis .
Walks are what give fids their meaning, so this follows them and keeps a
name for every fid.
.PP
Short printable writes are shown as text, because that is usually where
the interesting part of a session is \(em a Tk command, a line to a
control file \(em and a byte count says nothing about it.
.B -f
restricts the report to paths containing a substring, and
.B -b
ignores reads and writes below a size.
.SH EXAMPLE
.IP
.EX
% iostats -t /s.styx sh /tmp/session.sh
% styxlog /s.styx
    0.006  open   /dis/sh.dis
    0.012  open   /tmpsh/sess.sh
    0.013  open   /dis/echo.dis
    0.014  write  /dev/null: hello\en
    0.016  open   /dis/sleep.dis
    1.018  open   /dis/date.dis
    1.021  open   /dev/time
    1.022  write  /dev/null: Sun Aug 23 18:47:45 GMT 2026\en
.EE
.PP
Loading a module is an open of its
.B .dis
file, so the programs a session ran are in the log, and so is the pause
the sleep caused.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/styxlog.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR iostats (4),
.IR styxreplay (1)
.SH BUGS
A read is counted but not attributed to a path, because attributing it
needs the request it answers and this walks the trace in one pass.
Opens carry the path, which is usually enough to follow what happened.
.PP
It does not know which process did what.
A trace records one name space, and every process sharing it appears in
the same stream.
